Argo CD runs as a set of deployments inside your Kubernetes cluster.

Create a dedicated namespace for Argo CD:
<bash>
kubectl create namespace argocd
</bash>
Apply the Argo CD installation manifest directly from their official stable release:

<bash>
kubectl apply -n argocd -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/stable/manifests/install.yaml
</bash>
Wait for the pods to spin up. You can watch the progress with:

<bash>
kubectl get pods -n argocd -w
</bash>
Wait until all pods show a status of Running.

Step 2: Access the Argo CD UI
By default, the Argo CD API server is not exposed outside the cluster. To access it from your browser, you need to set up port-forwarding.

Run the port-forward command:

<bash>
kubectl port-forward svc/argocd-server -n argocd 8080:443
</bash>
(Leave this terminal window open. If you close it, you will lose access to the UI).

Retrieve the initial admin password. Argo CD automatically generates a password for the admin user and stores it in a Kubernetes secret. Open a new terminal window and run:

<bash>
kubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret -o jsonpath="{.data.password}" | base64 -d; echo
</bash>
Copy the output; you will need it in the next step.

Log in to the UI:

Open your browser and go to: https://localhost:8080 (Your browser will likely give you a self-signed certificate warning; you can safely bypass this for local testing).

